.TH loom-vault 1  "loom-vault 0.9.4"
.SH NAME
loom\-vault \- Credential vault: grant/revoke lifecycle + direct credential CRUD.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBloom\-vault\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommand\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The vault subsystem mediates OAuth token access for agent sessions and (as of
v0.9.4) provides a direct credential-management surface for storing,
inspecting, and deleting credentials in the operating-system keychain.
Two distinct flows live under \fBloom vault add\fR:

.IP \(bu 2
\fBOAuth device flow\fR: \fBloom vault add <provider>\fR \- the legacy v0.9.3
flow; \fIprovider\fR must be on the OAuth allowlist (\fBgithub\fR today).

.IP \(bu 2
\fBDirect credential injection\fR (v0.9.4): \fBloom vault add \-\-label <name>
\-\-from\-stdin\fR (or \fB\-\-from\-file <path>\fR). Reads raw bytes until EOF
(binary\-safe per A\-W6.2), validates \fI<name>\fR per D37, and stores via the
\fBvault.set_secret\fR RPC. Mutually exclusive with the positional \fIprovider\fR.

.SH SUBCOMMANDS

.TP
\fBloom vault grant\fR
Issue an opaque grant token bound to (session, origin, scopes, ttl).
The grant is the only handle WASM guests see.

.TP
\fBloom vault revoke\fR
Revoke an active grant by id; subsequent \fBsubstitute()\fR calls fail with
\fBVaultGrantRevoked\fR.

.TP
\fBloom vault list\fR
List alive grants. Distinct from \fBlist\-labels\fR which lists credentials.

.TP
\fBloom vault add\fR [\fB<provider>\fR] [\fB\-\-label <name>\fR]
       [\fB\-\-from\-stdin\fR | \fB\-\-from\-file <path>\fR]
       [\fB\-\-overwrite\fR] [\fB\-\-session <id>\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Store a credential. See DESCRIPTION for the two flows.
\fB\-\-overwrite\fR is required to replace an existing label (default refuses
with \fBAlreadyExists\fR exit code 5 per D33).
\fB\-\-session <id>\fR writes G5a/G5b audit entries to the named session's
manifest; sessionless (default) records via \fBtracing::info!\fR only.
\fB\-\-yes\fR skips interactive confirmation.

.TP
\fBloom vault delete <label>\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-session <id>\fR]
Delete a credential by label. Per D29: by default fails with
\fBVaultRejection { code: credential_in_use }\fR if any active grant references
\fI<label>\fR; with \fB\-\-force\fR, cascade\-revokes each referencing grant
(one \fBGrantRevoked{reason: "credential_deleted"}\fR audit per grant) before
removing the credential.

.TP
\fBloom vault list\-labels\fR [\fB\-\-session <id>\fR]
Enumerate stored credential labels under the daemon's service id (hardcoded
\fB"loom"\fR per D36 in v0.9.4). Output is a structured array safe for the JSON
pipe path. \fBNo per\-label byte data\fR \- only the labels themselves.

.TP
\fBloom vault diagnose\fR
Snapshot of the keychain backend's health + last\-error state. Stable JSON
schema per A\-W6.4 (see loom\-vault\-audit.md \(sc 3). Shape:
.RS
.PP
\fB{backend, init_status, service_id, label_count, last_keychain_error?}\fR
.RE
.PP
\fBjq\fR\-friendly across patch releases. Power users automate alerts against
\fB.last_keychain_error.kind\fR and feed \fB.internal_hash\fR into a daemon
log search to recover the original error message (see TROUBLESHOOTING).

.SH ENVIRONMENT

.TP
\fBLOOM_KEYCHAIN_BACKEND\fR
Backend override. One of \fBstub\fR, \fBin_memory\fR, \fBmacos\fR, \fBlinux\fR.
Defaults to the platform default: \fBmacos\fR on macOS, \fBlinux\fR on Linux,
\fBstub\fR otherwise. Set \fBstub\fR explicitly to diagnose backend init
failures (the daemon refuses to fall back silently per D7). \fBin_memory\fR is
intended for daemon\-level integration tests only \- the secret store is
process\-local and does NOT persist across restarts.

.TP
\fBLOOM_KEYCHAIN_ALLOW_PROMPT\fR
\fB1\fR or \fB0\fR. Whether the daemon may trigger OS unlock / biometric
prompts during keychain ops. Default: derived from the parent's TTY status
(\fB1\fR when stdin is a TTY, \fB0\fR otherwise). Backends that would have
prompted with this set to \fB0\fR return \fBNonInteractivePrompt\fR \- the
operator must unlock the keychain out of band before the daemon retries.

.TP
\fBKEYCHAIN_CI_REQUIRE_DAEMON\fR
CI\-only escape hatch read by the Linux backend integration tests. When set to
\fB1\fR (typically by the CI \fBgnome\-keyring\fR setup step \- see A\-W7.2),
\fBlinux_keychain_e2e.rs\fR tests fail loud on construction error instead of
self\-skipping. Operators should NOT set this; it's strictly a CI signal.

.SH EXIT CODES (per D33)

.TP
\fB0\fR
Success.

.TP
\fB1\fR
Generic failure (daemon\-side internal error, transport failure).

.TP
\fB2\fR
Not found (\fBvault delete\fR of an unknown label \- though this is idempotent
and currently returns 0). Label validation failures at the CLI boundary also
surface here as \fBCliError::Usage\fR.

.TP
\fB3\fR
Permission denied (keychain ACL refused, OS unlock prompt cancelled).

.TP
\fB4\fR
Invalid arguments (overlong / non\-canonical label per D37; non\-hex
\fBsecret_hex\fR; secret exceeds 1 MiB per A\-W6.2).

.TP
\fB5\fR
Already exists (\fBvault add\fR without \fB\-\-overwrite\fR for an existing
label).

.B Note:
v0.9.4 \fBpartial\fR mapping \- the wider \fBerror_mapper\fR table drifts from
D33 in some places (label\-validation failures emit exit 2, not 4). A full
strict re\-alignment is tracked for v0.9.5.

.SH TROUBLESHOOTING (per A\-W8.2 / FND-0057)

.TP
\fBI see "backend init failed" at daemon startup.\fR
The platform backend couldn't construct. On macOS this typically means the
login keychain is corrupt or the user is logged into the system but their
keychain is locked. On Linux it means \fBorg.freedesktop.secrets\fR has no
owner \- there's no running Secret Service provider on the session bus.
.IP
\fBDiagnostic only\fR: set \fBLOOM_KEYCHAIN_BACKEND=stub\fR to start the
daemon. \fBStubKeychain\fR returns \fBUnavailable\fR for every op so vault.*
RPCs all fail \- which is fine when you're just trying to confirm the daemon
boots and the failure is keychain\-localised.
.IP
\fBNot a recovery\fR: production must run with a real backend per D7.

.TP
\fBI'm on Linux and gnome\-keyring won't start.\fR
The login keyring is unlocked on first GUI login; running \fBloom\-daemon\fR
from a non\-graphical session (SSH, container, CI) usually requires:
.RS
.PP
.EX
eval "$(dbus\-launch \-\-sh\-syntax)"
echo "" | gnome\-keyring\-daemon \-\-unlock \-\-components=secrets \-\-daemonize
.EE
.RE
.IP
See CONTRIBUTING.md \(sc "Running Linux keychain integration tests" for the
full runbook.

.TP
\fBI got an "Unauthorized" / biometric prompt I didn't expect on macOS.\fR
Some macOS keychain items trigger Touch ID on each \fBSecItemCopyMatching\fR
when \fBkSecAccessControlBiometryAny\fR is set. v0.9.4 doesn't attach
\fBkSecAccessControl\fR programmatically \- if you see a prompt, an external
process (e.g. Keychain Access.app) added that ACL. Open Keychain Access.app,
find the loom item under \fBservice=loom\fR, and remove the biometric ACL.

.TP
\fBI see "AlreadyExists" but I want to replace the credential.\fR
Pass \fB\-\-overwrite\fR. In non\-interactive contexts (CI, scripts) also pass
\fB\-\-yes\fR to confirm replacement without prompting:
.RS
.PP
.EX
loom vault add \-\-label github\-token \-\-from\-stdin \-\-overwrite \-\-yes < new\-token.txt
.EE
.RE

.TP
\fBI need to recover the original message behind an internal_hash.\fR
\fBloom vault diagnose\fR's \fB.last_keychain_error.internal_hash\fR is a
SHA\-256 over the third\-party error message. The message itself is
\fBNEVER\fR persisted to any session manifest (G1). To recover it, search the
daemon's structured log:
.RS
.PP
.EX
grep <hash> ~/.local/share/loom/daemon.log
.EE
.RE
.IP
A\-W6.3 commits the daemon to emitting \fBtracing::error!(internal_hash =
<hash>, original_message = <msg>, ...)\fR at every \fBInternal\fR\-kind
failure, so the log line is the correlation target.

.SH FILES

.TP
\fB~/.local/share/loom/sessions/<sid>/manifest.{wal,jsonl}\fR
(macOS: \fB~/Library/Application Support/loom/sessions/<sid>/...\fR.) The
session's audit chain. Mode \fB0600\fR, owned by the daemon UID.

.TP
\fB~/.local/share/loom/auth/{hello.token,daemon.pid}\fR
Daemon auth artefacts. Mode \fB0600\fR \- the daemon refuses to start with
loose perms (A\-W8.1 / W8.5).

